const d=["Financial Foundations","Indian Stock Market Basics","How Stock Prices Work","Reading Financial Statements","Fundamental Analysis","Technical Analysis","Trading and Strategies","Long-Term Investing","Risk Management and Psychology","Futures and Options","Practical Indian Market Knowledge","Independent Stock Research"],l="https://investor.sebi.gov.in/",a="https://zerodha.com/varsity/module/fundamental-analysis/",s="https://zerodha.com/varsity/module/technical-analysis/",o="https://www.nseindia.com/",r=[{level:1,title:"Introduction to Investing",tool:"compound",meaning:"Saving sets aside money; investing commits money to an asset with an uncertain future benefit. A share represents partial ownership of a business. Your return can come from cash distributed as dividends and from a change in the price someone will pay for that ownership. Neither is assured. Before choosing a product, define the goal, the time available and the loss you can bear without disrupting essential spending.",why:"A long horizon can give a business time to grow, but it cannot rescue every weak business or an excessive purchase price. Separate money needed for near-term expenses and emergencies from money you can leave invested. Risk capacity depends on obligations and available cash, while risk tolerance describes how you feel about losses. Both affect a suitable plan.",analogy:"Buying a small part of a neighbourhood shop gives you exposure to its future profits. The share certificate is ownership, not a promise of a fixed payout.",example:"Synthetic example: ten shares bought at ₹100 cost ₹1,000. If the price becomes ₹110 and each share pays ₹2, the gross return is ₹120, or 12%, before costs and taxes.",formula:"Total return = (ending value − starting value + dividends) / starting value × 100",exercise:"List a one-year expense and a ten-year goal. Explain which could tolerate an uncertain stock-market value. In the growth lab, compare 5% and 10% assumptions over ten years.",mistake:"Treating a hypothetical calculator return as a guarantee, or investing emergency money in volatile shares.",research:"Write why the business may earn more in future, and what could cause a permanent loss. A rising chart alone does not explain business value.",question:"What does an equity share represent?",options:["Partial business ownership with uncertain returns","A guaranteed interest payment","An insurance policy"],explanation:"Shareholders participate in business outcomes and can lose capital. They do not receive a contractual fixed return."},{level:1,title:"Compounding and the cost of waiting",tool:"compound",meaning:"Compounding means returns can themselves earn subsequent returns. At a constant annual rate, each year multiplies the previous balance by the same factor. A percentage applied to a growing base produces larger rupee increments over time. This mathematical model is useful for planning scenarios; actual stock returns vary and may be negative.",why:"Time, contributions, fees and the rate all affect the ending balance. Small recurring costs reduce the amount available to compound. A geometric annual rate is different from the arithmetic average of annual returns: after +20% and −20%, ₹100 becomes ₹96, even though the arithmetic average is zero. Do not simply average volatile returns in a projection.",analogy:"A tree adds branches which can later add their own branches. Removing branches every year changes how much can grow next year.",example:"Synthetic: ₹10,000 at 10% annually becomes ₹11,000 after one year and ₹12,100 after two. The second year adds ₹1,100 because the first year’s ₹1,000 remains invested.",formula:"Future value = principal × (1 + annual rate)^years; CAGR = (end / start)^(1 / years) − 1",exercise:"Compare starting today with starting five years later at the same rate. Record the ending values and the return assumption. Then repeat with a lower rate.",mistake:"Confusing a 10% annual effective return with 10% each month, or ignoring withdrawals, fees and taxes.",research:"Use CAGR only for positive starting capital and a stated interval. Include dividends and cash flows appropriately before comparing performance.",question:"₹10,000 at 10% compounded annually is worth what after two years?",options:["₹12,100","₹12,000","₹20,000"],explanation:"₹10,000 × 1.1 × 1.1 = ₹12,100. The first year’s return also earns 10%."},{level:1,title:"Inflation, real returns and diversification",tool:"inflation",meaning:"Inflation reduces the purchasing power of a rupee. A nominal return measures the money gained; a real return measures the gain after the change in prices. Diversification spreads exposure across different economic drivers. Holding many nearly identical companies can still leave a portfolio highly exposed to the same risk.",why:"A bank balance can rise while buying less. Planning needs a realistic view of expenses and uncertainty rather than only a large future rupee number. Diversification can reduce company-specific risk, such as a failed product or fraud, but it cannot remove all broad market declines. Liquidity needs and investment horizons matter alongside expected returns.",analogy:"If your salary rises 5% while the grocery basket rises 8%, the larger payslip buys fewer baskets. Several umbrellas of the same fragile design do not protect against the same storm.",example:"Synthetic: 8% nominal growth and 6% inflation produce about 1.89% real growth, not exactly 2%. ₹1,00,000 of today’s spending would cost about ₹1,79,085 after ten years at 6% inflation.",formula:"Real return = (1 + nominal return) / (1 + inflation) − 1",exercise:"Use the inflation lab at 4%, 6% and 8%. Then describe a portfolio whose businesses depend on different sources of demand.",mistake:"Assuming inflation is constant or that adding stock names automatically provides meaningful diversification.",research:"Identify whether a business can pass rising costs to customers. Map sector, currency and customer concentration across holdings.",question:"An 8% nominal return with 6% inflation gives approximately:",options:["1.89% real return","14% real return","8% real return"],explanation:"1.08 / 1.06 − 1 = 0.01887. Inflation changes purchasing power rather than the stated account balance."},{level:2,title:"Shares, exchanges and market participants",tool:"allocation",meaning:"A company can divide its ownership into shares. In a primary issue, the company or selling shareholders offer shares; in secondary trading, existing holders exchange shares with other investors. NSE and BSE provide organised trading venues. A broker intermediates access, a depository maintains dematerialised securities, and SEBI regulates the securities market.",why:"Knowing which institution does what helps you verify an intermediary and understand where money and securities move. An exchange listing does not certify that a company is a good investment. Index membership is also not a guarantee. An IPO price and a later traded price reflect different transactions and may differ substantially.",analogy:"A marketplace provides a place to trade, a shopkeeper facilitates the transaction, and a records office tracks legal ownership. None promises that everything offered will increase in value.",example:"Synthetic: DemoCo has 10 lakh shares. Owning 1,000 shares represents 0.1% ownership before any dilution. Buying from another investor ordinarily does not send new capital to DemoCo.",formula:"Ownership fraction = shares owned / total shares outstanding",exercise:"Calculate the ownership fraction for 500 of 2,00,000 shares. Locate the registration details of an intermediary on official sources without sharing personal credentials.",mistake:"Thinking your secondary-market purchase directly funds the company, or treating a listed share as regulator-approved investment advice.",research:"Check the correct exchange identifier, ISIN and share count. Corporate actions can change share counts and per-share measures.",question:"Who generally receives money in a secondary-market share purchase?",options:["The selling investor","The company in every transaction","SEBI"],explanation:"Secondary trades transfer existing ownership. Fresh capital raising is a separate primary-market process."},{level:2,title:"Demat accounts and order types",tool:"order-book",meaning:"A trading account routes orders, while a demat account holds securities electronically. A market order seeks execution at available prices. A limit order constrains the price: a buy limit specifies the maximum willing payment; a sell limit specifies the minimum acceptable receipt. An order can remain unfilled or fill partly if matching liquidity is insufficient.",why:"Displayed last traded price is an observation, not a promise of execution. Costs, available depth and price movement can affect the realised purchase. Stop orders add a trigger condition, but a trigger does not guarantee execution at that exact price. Understand your broker’s supported order types, validity and handling of gaps before any real transaction.",analogy:"At a vegetable auction, saying “buy now” accepts current sellers’ prices. Saying “only at ₹50 or less” limits cost but may leave you without vegetables.",example:"Synthetic: asks are 100 shares at ₹101 and 200 at ₹102. A market buy for 150 consumes the first level and 50 at the second, giving average ₹101.33 before fees.",formula:"Average fill = sum(price × quantity filled) / total quantity filled",exercise:"Submit 50 then 250 shares in the order-book simulator. Compare fill price and unfilled quantity. Try a buy limit below the best ask.",mistake:"Assuming a stop-loss ensures a precise exit price or a limit order must execute because the chart briefly touched the limit.",research:"Check normal volume, spread and available depth before deciding whether an intended position is practical to transact.",question:"A buy limit at ₹100 permits a fill at:",options:["₹100 or lower","Any price above ₹100","Exactly ₹100 only"],explanation:"The buy limit is a maximum. It can execute at better prices, but execution is not assured."},{level:2,title:"Indices, settlement and corporate actions",tool:"comparison",meaning:"An index summarises a defined basket using published selection and weighting rules. A price index reflects price changes; a total-return index also accounts for reinvested distributions. Settlement is the post-trade process that exchanges funds and securities. Corporate actions such as splits, bonuses and dividends can change share quantities, reference prices or cash flows.",why:"A benchmark comparison is meaningful only when the periods and return definitions match. A stock split usually changes the number of shares and the unit price without creating economic value by itself. Unadjusted charts can show apparent jumps caused by actions rather than business performance. Settlement rules can evolve; consult the exchange’s current schedule for the relevant segment.",analogy:"Cutting a pizza into twice as many slices does not create a second pizza. Likewise, a two-for-one split can double shares while roughly halving the per-share price.",example:"Synthetic: 10 shares at ₹1,000 become 20 at a theoretical ₹500 after a two-for-one split. Total value remains ₹10,000 before market movements.",formula:"Theoretical split price = previous price / split factor",exercise:"Compare two series only after documenting dividend treatment and adjustments. Write the source and date of the settlement rule you consult.",mistake:"Calling an unadjusted split-price decline a 50% investment loss, or comparing a dividend-inclusive portfolio with a price-only benchmark.",research:"Keep an action log alongside imported prices. This release marks adjustment status and does not automatically repair corporate actions.",question:"A two-for-one split, before market movements, generally:",options:["Doubles quantity and halves theoretical price","Doubles total economic value","Removes half your ownership"],explanation:"The ownership fraction normally stays the same. Share count and per-share price change together."},{level:3,title:"Supply, demand and the bid–ask spread",tool:"order-book",meaning:"The best bid is the highest standing buying price; the best ask is the lowest standing selling price. The spread is their difference. Transactions occur when compatible orders meet. Price changes arise as participants revise orders or consume liquidity. A quoted order book is a temporary snapshot, not a complete view of everyone’s intentions.",why:"A wide spread creates an immediate trading friction. A large market order may walk through several levels, making its average fill differ from the best ask or bid. Thinly traded stocks can have large jumps even without a major change in fundamental value. Market depth can change before your order arrives.",analogy:"The highest offer at an auction and the lowest seller reservation define a negotiation gap. Taking all cheap offers leaves the next buyer facing higher prices.",example:"Synthetic: best bid ₹99 and ask ₹101 give a ₹2 spread. Buying at ₹101 and immediately selling at ₹99 loses about 1.98% before other costs.",formula:"Spread = best ask − best bid; relative spread = spread / mid-price",exercise:"Use the simulator to increase order size until several ask levels are consumed. Explain why last traded price is not a fill guarantee.",mistake:"Reading displayed liquidity as permanent or assuming every price move is evidence of an informed insider.",research:"Record spread and liquidity constraints as execution risks in your thesis, particularly when planned holdings are large relative to turnover.",question:"What is the spread when bid is ₹99 and ask is ₹101?",options:["₹2","₹200","₹1"],explanation:"The spread is ask minus bid. Crossing it has a cost even if the market does not move."},{level:3,title:"Volume, liquidity and volatility",tool:"indicators",meaning:"Volume counts shares traded over a defined interval. Liquidity describes how readily a position can be transacted without a large price concession. Volatility measures variation in returns, often with standard deviation. These concepts are related but distinct: high turnover does not promise a tight spread during a shock, and low historical volatility does not rule out a large future loss.",why:"Risk estimates depend on sampling frequency and history. Daily volatility multiplied by the square root of 252 is an annualisation convention, not a precise forecast. It assumes a statistical structure that actual markets may violate. Extreme outcomes, gaps and changing regimes can make backward-looking measures understate prospective risk.",analogy:"Traffic count measures how many cars pass; road capacity describes ease of movement; speed variation describes instability. One number does not capture all three.",example:"Synthetic: returns of +1%, −1%, +1%, −1% have near-zero average but nonzero volatility. A series growing smoothly by 1% daily would have low measured variability and still be unrealistic.",formula:"Daily return = close today / close yesterday − 1; annualised volatility ≈ daily standard deviation × √252",exercise:"Inspect the synthetic series and change the moving-average window. Identify high-volume days without claiming they caused subsequent price movements.",mistake:"Equating volatility with all risk, or interpreting a high-volume day as automatically bullish.",research:"Combine volatility with leverage, liquidity and permanent-loss scenarios. State the measurement period whenever you cite a risk statistic.",question:"Can returns average zero while still being volatile?",options:["Yes, gains and losses may offset","No, zero mean implies no movement","Only if volume is zero"],explanation:"The mean describes the centre; standard deviation describes spread around it."},{level:3,title:"Market capitalisation and price discovery",tool:"pe",meaning:"Market capitalisation is the traded price multiplied by shares outstanding. It measures market equity value, not the cost of all a business’s assets or its entire financing. Enterprise value adjusts equity value for items such as debt and cash, using a consistent definition. A low share price does not imply a small or inexpensive company.",why:"Share counts and business earnings matter when comparing valuations. A ₹20 stock can have a much larger equity value than a ₹2,000 stock. Price discovery aggregates changing beliefs, constraints and liquidity. A market price can be informative and still differ from your estimate of intrinsic value; your estimate can also be wrong.",analogy:"The price of one slice tells little about a bakery unless you know how many slices it sold and what it earns. Unit price is not business size.",example:"Synthetic: company A has 100 crore shares at ₹20, giving ₹2,000 crore market value. Company B has 50 lakh shares at ₹2,000, giving ₹1,000 crore.",formula:"Market capitalisation = share price × shares outstanding",exercise:"In the valuation lab, hold earnings fixed and double the share price. Observe how the price-to-earnings multiple changes.",mistake:"Ranking cheapness by share price, or mistaking equity market value for enterprise value.",research:"Check diluted share counts, debt, cash and minority interests before comparing company valuations.",question:"Which is needed alongside price to calculate market capitalisation?",options:["Shares outstanding","Yesterday’s volume only","Number of exchanges"],explanation:"Market capitalisation represents all outstanding equity units valued at the current price."},{level:4,title:"Income statements: from sales to earnings",tool:"statements",meaning:"The income statement reports economic performance during a period. Revenue records sales according to accounting rules; operating expenses, finance costs, taxes and other items bridge revenue to net profit. Accrual accounting means revenue can be recognised before cash arrives. Profitability therefore cannot be inferred just from bank receipts.",why:"Growing sales can coexist with falling profit if input costs, interest or discounts rise. One-off gains can inflate reported earnings without improving normal business performance. Compare like reporting periods, including seasonality, and distinguish consolidated results from standalone results. Banks and industrial companies use different statement conventions. Weighted-average diluted shares are needed for comparable EPS; a year-end share count is only a simplified denominator.",analogy:"A restaurant’s daily takings are not its owner’s earnings. Ingredients, wages, rent, loan interest and taxes still need to be paid.",example:"Synthetic DemoCo earns ₹1.2 crore revenue and ₹15 lakh net profit: a 12.5% net margin. If revenue rises but profit stays flat, margin falls.",formula:"Net margin = net profit / revenue × 100; EPS = attributable profit / weighted-average shares",exercise:"Change revenue and profit in the statement lab. Calculate margin and identify whether growth improves earnings per share.",mistake:"Treating revenue as cash received, mixing quarterly and annual figures, or ignoring one-time items.",research:"Read notes about revenue recognition and exceptional items. Compare recurring operating outcomes across several periods.",question:"Revenue of ₹100 and net profit of ₹12 give a margin of:",options:["12%","88%","8.33%"],explanation:"Net margin measures the portion of revenue remaining as net profit: 12 / 100.",reference:a},{level:4,title:"Balance sheets and financial resilience",tool:"ratios",meaning:"A balance sheet describes assets, liabilities and equity at a point in time. Assets are resources controlled by the business; liabilities are obligations; equity is the residual claim after liabilities. A balance sheet is a snapshot rather than a period’s flow. Book values follow accounting policies and may differ substantially from saleable market values.",why:"A profitable company can be fragile if its funding must be refinanced quickly or its assets are difficult to convert to cash. Debt-to-equity compares one type of obligation with the equity buffer, but it is not a complete solvency test. Negative equity makes conventional ratio interpretation problematic. For banks, deposits and regulatory capital require specialist analysis.",analogy:"A household can own a valuable house and still struggle to pay next month’s bills. Wealth, cash availability and repayment schedules are separate concerns.",example:"Synthetic: assets of ₹80 lakh financed by ₹30 lakh liabilities leave ₹50 lakh equity. If interest-bearing debt is ₹20 lakh, debt-to-equity is 0.4 times.",formula:"Assets = liabilities + equity; debt-to-equity = interest-bearing debt / equity",exercise:"In the ratio lab, double debt while holding equity constant. Describe the extra interest and refinancing risk that the ratio alone cannot quantify.",mistake:"Comparing debt-to-equity across unrelated sectors or treating every asset as immediately available cash.",research:"Review maturity schedules, contingent liabilities, receivables and auditor notes. Use average equity for return ratios when appropriate.",question:"Assets of ₹80 and liabilities of ₹30 imply equity of:",options:["₹50","₹110","₹30"],explanation:"Equity is the residual: assets minus liabilities.",reference:a},{level:4,title:"Cash flows and earnings quality",tool:"statements",meaning:"The cash-flow statement groups cash movement into operating, investing and financing activities. Operating cash flow can differ from net profit because of working capital, non-cash costs and timing. Free cash flow is commonly estimated as operating cash flow minus capital expenditure; definitions vary, so state the one used.",why:"A company cannot pay suppliers indefinitely with accounting profit. Rising receivables may consume cash as sales grow. Heavy investment can reduce current free cash flow while building useful capacity, or it can destroy value. One year of weak cash conversion is not enough to conclude fraud, but persistent gaps deserve investigation.",analogy:"An invoice is a claim; cash collection is money in the till. A shop may record a sale today while waiting months to be paid.",example:"Synthetic DemoCo records ₹16 lakh operating cash flow and ₹5 lakh capex, giving ₹11 lakh simple free cash flow. Borrowing ₹8 lakh raises cash but is financing, not operating success.",formula:"Simple free cash flow = operating cash flow − capital expenditure",exercise:"Raise capex in the statement lab. Explain why lower free cash flow could reflect either productive investment or a deteriorating business.",mistake:"Counting fresh borrowing as business-generated cash or overlooking cash spent maintaining existing capacity.",research:"Track operating cash flow against earnings over a multi-year cycle, and inspect working-capital changes and capex disclosures.",question:"OCF of ₹16 lakh and capex of ₹5 lakh produce simple FCF of:",options:["₹11 lakh","₹21 lakh","₹5 lakh"],explanation:"This defined FCF measure subtracts capital expenditure from operating cash flow.",reference:a}];r.push({level:5,title:"P/E, P/B and the meaning of cheap",tool:"pe",meaning:"Price-to-earnings divides share price by earnings per share. It asks how much equity investors pay for one unit of annual earnings. Price-to-book divides price by book equity per share. These ratios compress information; they do not explain the durability of earnings, the quality of assets or the risk of the business. A negative earnings denominator makes a conventional P/E uninformative.",why:"Low multiples can reflect undervaluation, but also declining profits, governance problems or temporary peak earnings. High multiples can reflect growth expectations that may disappoint. Compare the same earnings period and accounting perimeter. Bank valuations require attention to asset quality and capital; industrial valuations require different operating measures.",analogy:"A house’s price per square foot is a starting comparison, not a verdict. Condition, location, repairs and future use still matter.",example:"Synthetic: price ₹150 and annual EPS ₹10 give P/E 15. If sustainable EPS falls to ₹5, the same price implies P/E 30 even though the ticker has not moved.",formula:"P/E = price / annual EPS; P/B = price / book equity per share",exercise:"Change price and EPS independently. Find two combinations with P/E 15 and explain why the businesses could still have different risks.",mistake:"Mixing quarterly EPS with annual price multiples or assuming a lower multiple always offers a better opportunity.",research:"Document trailing versus forward earnings, dilution, exceptional items and peer comparability before interpreting a valuation gap.",question:"Price ₹150 with annual EPS ₹10 implies:",options:["P/E 15","P/E 150","A guaranteed 15% return"],explanation:"15 is a valuation multiple, not an investment return or a promise.",reference:a},{level:5,title:"Profitability, leverage and growth quality",tool:"ratios",meaning:"Return on equity relates profit attributable to equity holders to the equity committed. A common analyst convention uses average opening and closing equity. Return on capital employed relates operating earnings to a stated measure of capital employed. Debt can amplify equity returns while adding financing risk. Revenue growth alone does not establish value creation.",why:"Compare ratios over time using consistent definitions. Buybacks, losses, acquisitions and changing leverage can alter denominators. A high ROE with a tiny or negative equity base can mislead. Growth funded by costly debt or persistent share issuance may leave existing shareholders worse off. ROCE is especially sensitive to the capital definition and treatment of cash.",analogy:"Two shops earning the same profit may require very different amounts of invested capital. The one using less capital is not automatically safer if it borrowed heavily.",example:"Synthetic: ₹15 lakh profit over average equity ₹55 lakh gives ROE 27.27%. Using year-end equity ₹60 lakh gives 25%; label the denominator rather than treating both as identical.",formula:"ROE = attributable profit / average equity × 100; debt-to-equity = debt / equity",exercise:"Change debt and equity in the ratio lab. Explain how a rising return ratio might coincide with rising financial risk.",mistake:"Using high ROE as a standalone quality score, or comparing differently defined ROCE figures across providers.",research:"Separate organic growth from acquisitions and identify reinvestment needs. The workbench labels simplified year-end equity ratios.",question:"Which change can raise ROE without improving operating profit?",options:["A smaller equity denominator","A larger share register alone","A longer company name"],explanation:"Leverage, buybacks or losses can shrink equity. Always inspect numerator and denominator.",reference:a},{level:5,title:"Valuation scenarios and margin of safety",tool:"pe",meaning:"Valuation is an estimate based on future economics and a required return. A discounted cash-flow model values future cash flows in today’s rupees. A multiple-based scenario estimates value from an earnings assumption and a valuation multiple. Both are sensitive to uncertain inputs. Margin of safety is a discount to your estimate, intended to allow for error; it cannot make a flawed estimate reliable.",why:"One precise price target hides a wide range of possible outcomes. Separate bull, base and bear assumptions about growth, profitability, capital needs and investor expectations. An attractive business can be an unattractive investment at an excessive price. Terminal assumptions often dominate long-duration models and deserve explicit stress tests.",analogy:"Estimating an orchard’s value requires thinking about future harvests, maintenance and weather, not just today’s fruit price.",example:"Synthetic: future EPS scenarios ₹8, ₹12 and ₹16 combined with P/E assumptions 10, 15 and 20 imply ₹80, ₹180 and ₹320 scenario values. These are illustrative assumptions, not targets.",formula:"Scenario equity value per share = assumed annual EPS × assumed P/E; PV = future cash flow / (1 + discount rate)^years",exercise:"Create a bear case with lower earnings and a lower multiple. Record what evidence would make that outcome plausible in Decision Lab.",mistake:"Selecting assumptions that justify a desired purchase or calling a model output a guaranteed future price.",research:"Save assumptions, uncertainty and invalidation conditions with the thesis. Update estimates after new financial disclosures.",question:"A valuation estimate is best treated as:",options:["An assumption-sensitive range","A guaranteed exit price","A substitute for financial statements"],explanation:"Valuation depends on uncertain cash flows and required returns. Scenarios reveal that uncertainty.",reference:a},{level:6,title:"Reading candlesticks without telling stories",tool:"candlestick",meaning:"A candle summarises open, high, low and close for one interval. Its body spans open to close; wicks extend to the high and low. Colour usually distinguishes a close above or below the open. A candle omits the order in which the high and low occurred and all individual trades. It is a compressed observation, not a complete narrative.",why:"The same daily candle can contain many different intraday paths. A visual pattern should therefore be interpreted with context and tested evidence. Timeframe matters: a weekly candle aggregates several daily intervals. Corporate actions and bad data can create false patterns. A doji or long wick alone cannot prove that a trend must reverse.",analogy:"A day’s weather summary gives the maximum, minimum and closing temperature but does not tell you exactly when the rain began.",example:"Synthetic: open ₹100, high ₹110, low ₹95, close ₹106 forms a green body from ₹100 to ₹106 with upper and lower wicks. The candle’s range is ₹15.",formula:"Body size = |close − open|; range = high − low",exercise:"Move the open and close sliders in the anatomy explorer. Keep high above both and low below both. Describe information the candle still cannot reveal.",mistake:"Assuming candle shape establishes the next price move or ignoring whether prices are split-adjusted.",research:"Use validated OHLCV and document the interval. Treat patterns as hypotheses to evaluate, not instructions to buy.",question:"Does one OHLC candle reveal whether the high occurred before the low?",options:["No","Always","Only when green"],explanation:"OHLC summarises values and does not preserve the full intraday sequence.",reference:s},{level:6,title:"Moving averages, trends and support",tool:"moving-average",meaning:"A simple moving average is the mean of the most recent N prices. An exponential moving average puts more weight on recent observations. Both smooth noise and lag new information. Support and resistance are hypothesised price regions where buying or selling has previously been important. They are not physical barriers and can break abruptly.",why:"Short averages react quickly but can generate frequent reversals; long averages respond slowly and may miss early changes. A crossover is a rule derived from past prices, not an independent explanation of future returns. Choosing a support level after seeing the full chart introduces hindsight. Decide level rules using only information available at the time.",analogy:"A moving average is like smoothing uneven daily temperature readings: it reveals a broader pattern while responding late to a sudden cold spell.",example:"Synthetic closes ₹100, ₹102 and ₹104 have a three-period SMA of ₹102. Adding ₹110 drops the oldest value: the next SMA is ₹105.33.",formula:"SMA = sum(last N closes) / N; EMA weight α = 2 / (N + 1)",exercise:"Compare 20- and 50-period windows. Then draw a support line and list a rule for invalidating it before inspecting later prices.",mistake:"Treating moving averages as forecasts or choosing the best-looking period after examining all outcomes.",research:"Use averages to describe trend context and combine them with cost-aware out-of-sample testing.",question:"What happens when the SMA period increases?",options:["It generally smooths more and reacts more slowly","It guarantees fewer losing trades","It adds future data"],explanation:"A larger window averages more history. This reduces responsiveness without guaranteeing profitability.",reference:s},{level:6,title:"RSI, MACD, Bollinger Bands and ATR",tool:"indicators",meaning:"RSI compares smoothed gains and losses on a 0–100 scale. MACD is the difference between fast and slow EMAs, with a signal EMA. Bollinger Bands place standard-deviation envelopes around an average. ATR smooths true range, including gaps from the previous close. These measures describe different aspects of the same underlying price series.",why:"Several indicators agreeing may just reflect shared inputs, not independent evidence. RSI can stay high in a strong uptrend. Band breaches do not necessarily imply reversal. ATR measures range, not direction. Warm-up history and smoothing conventions affect values; this lab uses SMA-seeded EMAs and Wilder smoothing for RSI and ATR.",analogy:"A thermometer, average temperature and variability band describe the same weather from different angles. Counting them as three independent forecasts exaggerates confidence.",example:"Synthetic: fourteen consecutive gains with no losses give RSI 100. A completely flat series gives RSI 50 by this lab’s explicit convention; another provider may choose a different edge-case display.",formula:"RSI = 100 − 100 / (1 + average gain / average loss); MACD = EMA12 − EMA26",exercise:"Inspect the RSI and MACD panes. Change the SMA period and compare smoothing. Find an indicator condition that persists instead of immediately reversing.",mistake:"Calling RSI above 70 an automatic sell signal or plotting zero when an indicator lacks enough history.",research:"State parameters and conventions. Indicators appear only after their required history and never use widget-internal values.",question:"ATR principally measures:",options:["Price range including gaps","Business quality","Guaranteed trend direction"],explanation:"True range incorporates the high-low range and gaps relative to the previous close.",reference:s},{level:7,title:"Risk, reward and position sizing",tool:"position-sizing",meaning:"Position sizing links the amount exposed to the loss planned per share. For a long position, the entry minus stop is the planned price risk. Divide the money risk budget by that distance to estimate quantity, then respect the available capital constraint. Stops are planning levels; gaps, slippage and costs can produce a larger realised loss.",why:"A good-looking chart with an oversized position can overwhelm a portfolio. The reward-to-risk ratio compares a hypothetical target gain with a planned stop loss. It says nothing about how often the target is reached. Expected outcome depends on win rate, average gains, average losses and costs together.",analogy:"A safety budget limits the damage one failed experiment can cause. It does not make the experiment certain to succeed.",example:"Synthetic: capital ₹1,00,000, risk budget 1%, entry ₹100 and stop ₹95 imply at most 200 shares by stop-distance risk, before costs. A ₹110 target gives reward-to-risk 2.",formula:"Quantity = floor(capital × risk fraction / (entry − stop)); reward/risk = (target − entry) / (entry − stop)",exercise:"Halve the stop distance and observe the larger computed position. Explain why tighter stops do not automatically reduce actual risk.",mistake:"Ignoring correlated positions, gaps or slippage, and assuming a 2:1 target ratio guarantees positive expectancy.",research:"Record aggregate risk and liquidity constraints as well as individual planned losses.",question:"With ₹1,000 risk budget and ₹5 planned risk per share, quantity is:",options:["200 shares before capital constraints","5 shares","1,000 shares"],explanation:"₹1,000 / ₹5 = 200. Affordability and execution risks still apply."},{level:7,title:"Trend following, breakouts and false signals",tool:"support",meaning:"Trend following attempts to participate in persistent directional moves using predeclared rules. A breakout rule responds when price crosses a chosen range boundary. Range trading assumes price tends to revert within a region. The same market can favour one approach in one period and another later. Strategy labels are not evidence of profitability.",why:"Define entry, exit, sizing, cost assumptions and the conditions in which the idea should work. A breakout can fail as liquidity disappears or sellers return. Waiting for confirmation may reduce some false signals while worsening entry price. Intraday, swing and positional trading differ in holding periods, operational demands and transaction frictions.",analogy:"Following a river downstream works until the channel changes; a bridge painted on yesterday’s map may no longer exist.",example:"Synthetic: a rule enters only after a daily close exceeds the previous 20-day high, with execution no earlier than the next bar. Using that same day’s low as an already known fill would be hindsight.",formula:"Signal at time t may use data through t; simulated execution must respect when that signal became observable",exercise:"Draw a resistance level using an early chart segment. Write the entry and invalidation rule. Inspect the later segment without changing the original level.",mistake:"Moving support after every loss or counting a close-based signal as filled earlier during the same session.",research:"Save the rule before examining outcomes, and log false signals alongside successes.",question:"A rule based on today’s closing price is first fully observable:",options:["After today’s close","At yesterday’s open","Before today’s opening auction"],explanation:"Using the final close earlier introduces information that was not yet available."},{level:7,title:"Backtesting, slippage and honest evidence",tool:"risk-reward",meaning:"A backtest applies a stated rule to historical data. It can expose mechanical errors and explore behaviour, but it cannot prove future performance. Costs include brokerage and other charges; slippage is the difference between a reference price and realised execution. Survivorship bias excludes failed or delisted securities; look-ahead bias uses information unavailable at the decision time.",why:"Testing many parameter combinations and keeping only the winner overfits noise. Separate development data from validation data and preserve an untouched evaluation period. Include corporate actions and realistic fills. Report drawdown, turnover, sample size and regimes, not just a winning percentage. This release offers calculators and paper journaling rather than a full historical backtest engine.",analogy:"Practising on an exam’s answer sheet is not evidence that you can solve a new exam. A fair test withholds the answers.",example:"Synthetic: 60 wins of ₹100 and 40 losses of ₹120 yield ₹1,200 before costs. ₹15 cost per trade turns the 100-trade result into a ₹300 loss.",formula:"Expectancy = win probability × average win − loss probability × average loss − average costs",exercise:"Enter win rate 60%, gain ₹100, loss ₹120 and cost ₹15 in the risk lab. Explain why the majority of winning trades still loses money.",mistake:"Ignoring discarded trials, using today’s index constituents throughout history, or reporting simulations as real returns.",research:"Keep the complete experiment log and evaluate uncertainty before drawing conclusions.",question:"Using future financial results in an earlier simulated trade creates:",options:["Look-ahead bias","Diversification","More accurate execution"],explanation:"The simulated decision could not have known those results at that time."},{level:8,title:"SIP versus lump sum and investment discipline",tool:"sip",meaning:"A systematic investment plan spreads contributions across dates. A lump sum invests available capital at one time. SIP is a contribution method, not an asset class or a promise of returns. Regular purchases buy more units at lower prices and fewer at higher prices, but the ending value still depends on the asset’s path and future value.",why:"Recurring contributions can fit income and behavioural needs. Delaying available money also leaves it out of the market, which may help or hurt depending on subsequent prices. Comparing methods requires identical cash-flow assumptions and opportunities. This lab uses month-end contributions and an effective annual return converted to a monthly rate.",analogy:"Buying groceries weekly spreads purchase dates, but it does not ensure the items become more valuable or that the total cost is always lower.",example:"Synthetic: ₹1,000 at each month-end for one year with zero return creates ₹12,000. With a positive return, earlier contributions grow for longer than later ones.",formula:"SIP future value = contribution × ((1 + monthly rate)^months − 1) / monthly rate",exercise:"Set the SIP rate to zero, then 8%. Compare contributions with gains. Explain why a lump sum of all future contributions assumes money available earlier.",mistake:"Claiming SIP removes market risk or treating annual rate divided by 12 as always equivalent to effective annual compounding.",research:"Choose asset exposure and valuation discipline separately from contribution frequency.",question:"At zero return, ₹1,000 monthly for 12 months becomes:",options:["₹12,000","₹13,000","₹1,000"],explanation:"There is no growth at zero return; ending value equals total contributions."},{level:8,title:"ETFs, index funds and portfolio allocation",tool:"allocation",meaning:"An index fund aims to track a benchmark; an ETF trades on an exchange and may track an index or another specified exposure. Fees, tracking difference, liquidity and portfolio construction affect results. Allocation decides how capital is distributed among assets. It usually matters more for overall exposure than selecting many stock names within one sector.",why:"Diversification does not prevent market-wide losses. Equity and debt respond to different risks, and even supposedly defensive assets carry interest-rate, credit or liquidity risks. Index concentration can be substantial. An ETF’s market price can differ from its underlying net asset value; trading spread is an additional cost.",analogy:"A mixed garden spreads dependence across crops, but a severe drought can still affect the whole garden. Different crops reduce some risks, not every risk.",example:"Synthetic: ₹1 lakh split 60% equity, 30% debt and 10% cash has ₹60,000, ₹30,000 and ₹10,000 exposures. If equity falls 20% and the others stay flat, portfolio loss is 12%.",formula:"Portfolio return for a single period = sum(starting weight × asset return)",exercise:"Change equity weight in the allocation simulator and stress equity at −20%. Keep weights summing to 100%.",mistake:"Assuming an index fund is risk-free or counting several funds holding the same stocks as independent diversification.",research:"Inspect underlying holdings, benchmark methodology, costs and concentration before interpreting a portfolio label.",question:"A 60% equity weight with equity down 20% and other assets flat gives:",options:["−12% portfolio return","−20% portfolio return","+12% portfolio return"],explanation:"0.60 × −20% = −12%. The starting weights determine this single-period result."},{level:8,title:"Rebalancing and the long-term review process",tool:"allocation",meaning:"Rebalancing restores a portfolio towards a target allocation after price changes or new contributions. It can be calendar-based or triggered by a threshold. It is a risk-control process, not a guarantee of extra return. Business review checks whether a company’s economics still support the original thesis; price movement alone is a different question.",why:"An initially modest exposure can become concentrated after strong gains. Selling has costs and possible tax consequences. New contributions may reduce the need to sell. A rigid rebalance rule can be inappropriate if obligations or risk capacity have changed. Review the target itself periodically, not just the distance from it.",analogy:"A ship’s load can shift during a voyage. Redistributing it restores balance, but the correct load still depends on destination and weather.",example:"Synthetic: ₹60,000 equity and ₹40,000 debt become ₹90,000 and ₹40,000. Equity weight is now 69.23%. A 60% target on ₹1,30,000 implies ₹78,000 equity before transaction costs.",formula:"Current weight = asset value / total portfolio value; target value = target weight × total value",exercise:"Use allocation scenarios to compare target and current exposures. Write a review interval and a drift threshold before changing holdings.",mistake:"Rebalancing every small movement without considering costs, or confusing portfolio maintenance with a reason to keep a broken business thesis.",research:"Store review dates, allocation rules and thesis invalidation conditions in the journal.",question:"What is the principal purpose of rebalancing?",options:["Restoring intended risk exposure","Guaranteeing higher returns","Eliminating taxes"],explanation:"Rebalancing changes exposure towards a chosen target. Returns and costs remain uncertain."});r.push({level:9,title:"Drawdowns and recovery mathematics",tool:"drawdown",meaning:"A drawdown is the fall from a previous peak to the current value. Maximum drawdown is the deepest such fall in the observed period. It depends on both the path and the sample window. A large loss needs a disproportionately large percentage gain to recover because the gain applies to a smaller base.",why:"Return averages can hide difficult periods. A strategy may finish profitably while experiencing a loss that an investor cannot withstand. Historical maximum drawdown is not an upper bound on future loss. Leverage and withdrawals can make recovery even harder. Compare strategies with consistent valuation frequency and include cash flows correctly.",analogy:"Walking halfway down a hill does not mean a 50% climb from your new altitude returns you to the original height; the percentage base has changed.",example:"Synthetic: ₹1 lakh falling to ₹70,000 is a 30% drawdown. Recovery requires ₹30,000 / ₹70,000 = 42.86%. A 50% loss needs a 100% gain.",formula:"Drawdown = current value / previous peak − 1; recovery gain = loss fraction / (1 − loss fraction)",exercise:"Try losses of 10%, 30%, 50% and 80%. Record required recovery gains and decide which losses would interrupt a real-world financial goal.",mistake:"Assuming equal percentage gains and losses cancel or that historical drawdown defines the worst possible future event.",research:"Write a portfolio-level stress scenario, including the possibility that several holdings decline together.",question:"A 50% loss needs what gain to recover?",options:["100%","50%","25%"],explanation:"A value of 100 falls to 50. Returning to 100 requires a gain of 50 on a base of 50."},{level:9,title:"Leverage, concentration and risk budgets",tool:"allocation",meaning:"Leverage creates exposure larger than the equity committed, through borrowing or derivative contracts. It magnifies gains and losses and can introduce financing or margin obligations. Concentration links outcomes to a few businesses or common economic drivers. A risk budget states how much loss or exposure a decision may contribute to the overall portfolio.",why:"Individual positions that appear small can share the same interest-rate, sector or funding risks. A leveraged investor may be forced to sell at an unfavourable time. Static stop-distance budgets do not capture gap losses or correlated shocks. Household liabilities and income exposure should be considered alongside investment positions.",analogy:"Several boats tied to the same anchor are not independent. Borrowing a bigger boat also means a larger bill if the voyage fails.",example:"Synthetic: ₹1 lakh equity plus ₹1 lakh debt buys ₹2 lakh exposure. A 20% asset decline loses ₹40,000, or 40% of equity before interest. A larger fall can exhaust equity.",formula:"Exposure leverage = gross asset exposure / equity; approximate equity return = leverage × asset return before financing costs",exercise:"Stress an equity-heavy allocation, then double exposure conceptually. Identify which obligations remain even after the asset falls.",mistake:"Calling correlated holdings diversified or sizing each trade independently without checking total portfolio risk.",research:"Map sector, counterparty, borrowing and liquidity dependencies. Record loss limits as planning assumptions rather than guarantees.",question:"At 2× exposure, a 20% asset fall implies approximately:",options:["40% equity loss before costs","10% equity loss","No loss until sale"],explanation:"The loss is measured on the full exposure but absorbed by a smaller equity base."},{level:9,title:"FOMO, loss aversion and decision journals",tool:"checklist",meaning:"Fear of missing out can turn another person’s gain into urgency. Loss aversion makes a loss feel more painful than an equal gain feels rewarding. Confirmation bias favours evidence supporting an existing view. A decision journal records the reasoning and information available before the result, separating decision quality from luck.",why:"A profitable outcome does not prove a sound process, and a loss does not automatically disprove one. Prewritten invalidation conditions reduce the temptation to move the goalposts. Seeking disconfirming evidence and slowing impulsive decisions can improve consistency. A journal is useful only if entries are honest, dated and reviewed.",analogy:"A scientist writes the hypothesis before running the experiment. Rewriting it after seeing results makes every outcome look like a success.",example:"Synthetic: an investor buys because a friend reports a 40% gain. A better research entry first states the business case, valuation assumptions, possible losses and evidence that would invalidate the thesis.",formula:"Decision review: information then → assumptions → action → outcome → process lessons",exercise:"Use the checklist to identify three unanswered questions. Draft a journal entry and include one fact that would change your mind.",mistake:"Deleting failed ideas, treating sunk purchase cost as a reason to hold, or adding only evidence that supports an existing position.",research:"Return to the original note after new results. Compare predictions with evidence without judging solely by short-term price.",question:"Which practice helps counter confirmation bias?",options:["Actively searching for contrary evidence","Reading only bullish opinions","Changing the thesis after every outcome"],explanation:"A falsifiable thesis identifies evidence that would weaken or overturn the original view."},{level:10,title:"Futures, margin and contract obligations",tool:"position-sizing",meaning:"A futures contract creates an obligation tied to a specified underlying, contract size and expiry. Margin is collateral against potential loss, not the full economic exposure or a maximum loss. Gains and losses can generate additional funding requirements. Contract specifications, settlement method and expiry calendars are product-specific and may change.",why:"A modest price movement on a large notional contract can have a large effect on capital. Investors must understand daily settlement, liquidity, margin changes and expiry obligations. This educational platform never places real broker orders. It does not model futures margin or physical settlement, so a calculator result cannot establish affordability of a derivative position.",analogy:"A deposit on a large obligation is not the obligation’s total size. Paying a small booking amount does not limit what you might owe.",example:"Synthetic contract: 100 units at ₹1,000 create ₹1 lakh notional. A ₹50 adverse move loses ₹5,000 regardless of whether initial collateral was ₹10,000 or another amount.",formula:"Notional = contract price × lot size × contracts; P/L = price change × lot size × contracts",exercise:"Compare the ₹5,000 loss with ₹10,000 collateral. Consult current NSE contract specifications and list information missing from this synthetic example.",mistake:"Confusing margin paid with maximum potential loss or using an old lot size or expiry rule.",research:"Use current exchange specifications for the exact contract. Derivatives can cause substantial losses and additional obligations.",question:"Does margin necessarily cap a futures loss?",options:["No","Yes, in every contract","Only if the position is long"],explanation:"Margin is collateral. Adverse movement can require additional funds and losses may exceed the initial amount.",reference:"https://www.nseindia.com/static/products-services/equity-derivatives-contract-specifications"},{level:10,title:"Calls, puts and expiry payoff",tool:"options",meaning:"A call gives its buyer a right associated with buying the underlying at a strike; a put gives a right associated with selling at a strike, subject to the contract’s settlement terms. The buyer pays premium. At expiry, intrinsic value depends on spot and strike. Before expiry, option price also reflects time, volatility and other factors.",why:"Payoff diagrams isolate expiry economics and are not forecasts of tradable option prices. Long options can lose the entire premium. Short options can have large losses; an uncovered short call has theoretically unbounded loss. Lot sizes, costs, settlement obligations and margins can alter practical outcomes. This lab plots per-unit long-option expiry P/L only.",analogy:"Paying for a reservation right is different from buying the item. The right may become useful or expire unused, but the reservation fee remains spent.",example:"Synthetic call: strike ₹100, premium ₹8 and expiry spot ₹115 give ₹15 intrinsic value and ₹7 net profit per unit before costs. At ₹105 spot, net profit is −₹3.",formula:"Long call P/L = max(spot − strike, 0) − premium; long put P/L = max(strike − spot, 0) − premium",exercise:"Move spot across the strike in the payoff lab. Locate call breakeven and compare intrinsic value with net profit.",mistake:"Ignoring premium, assuming in-the-money means profitable, or using an expiry diagram as a pre-expiry price model.",research:"Record contract specifications, premium and settlement risk. Keep this learning exercise separate from actual trading.",question:"A long call at strike ₹100 with ₹8 premium breaks even at expiry at:",options:["₹108 before costs","₹100","₹92"],explanation:"The underlying must cover strike plus premium for this per-unit call calculation.",reference:o},{level:10,title:"Greeks, time decay and volatility risk",tool:"options",meaning:"Option Greeks describe local sensitivity in a pricing model. Delta relates option-price changes to underlying changes; gamma describes delta’s sensitivity; theta describes sensitivity to time; vega to implied volatility. They are estimates under model assumptions, not constants or guarantees. Their effects interact and can change sharply near expiry or the strike.",why:"Correctly predicting direction does not ensure option profitability. A fall in implied volatility or the passage of time can offset a favourable underlying move. Small-change sensitivity estimates can fail for large jumps. A payoff chart shows expiry outcomes and does not calculate Greeks or pre-expiry values in this release.",analogy:"A car’s steering response depends on speed and road conditions. One local sensitivity measured on a straight road does not describe every turn.",example:"Synthetic: delta 0.5 suggests an approximately ₹1 option change for a small ₹2 underlying move, holding other factors fixed. If time and implied volatility change, that approximation may no longer explain the actual result.",formula:"Local approximation: option change ≈ delta × spot change, with other inputs fixed",exercise:"Use the expiry payoff lab to see its limited information. Write why two calls with the same strike but different expiries can have different prices today.",mistake:"Treating Greeks as fixed, ignoring volatility crush, or assuming a long option must profit when the underlying moves in the desired direction.",research:"Document pricing inputs and liquidity. More advanced work requires a validated model and verified contract data.",question:"Can a long call lose value while the underlying rises?",options:["Yes, time and volatility changes can offset direction","No","Only if the exchange closes"],explanation:"Option value depends on more than underlying direction. Time value and implied volatility matter.",reference:o},{level:11,title:"Annual reports, quarterly results and filings",tool:"statements",meaning:"An annual report combines audited financial statements, notes and management discussion. Quarterly results update a shorter period and may have different assurance. Exchange filings communicate events and disclosures. The reporting period, publication date and accounting scope must be recorded separately. Publication timing determines when information became available for a decision.",why:"Headlines can omit important notes about exceptional items, related-party transactions or contingencies. Management commentary is useful context but should be checked against financial evidence. Comparing a consolidated annual figure with a standalone quarter is misleading. Restatements can alter earlier figures, which matters for historical research.",analogy:"A report card is more useful when you know the grading period, subjects and examiner. A headline mark without that context can mislead.",example:"Synthetic: a result for the quarter ended June is published in July. A simulated June decision cannot use the July disclosure, even though the result describes June operations.",formula:"Evidence record = company + reporting period + publication date + scope + units + source",exercise:"Find one annual report through the company’s investor-relations page or exchange filings. Record scope, units, audit opinion and publication date in a journal note.",mistake:"Using a reporting-period end as if it were the disclosure date or relying only on promotional summaries.",research:"Read footnotes and reconcile profit, cash flow and balance-sheet changes. Imports must retain known units and periods.",question:"June results published in July could be used in a June backtest decision:",options:["Only with look-ahead bias, so they must not be used","Without any timing concern","Whenever profit is positive"],explanation:"Publication date governs availability. Reporting-period dates do not make undisclosed information observable.",reference:o},{level:11,title:"Costs, tax records and changing rules",tool:"risk-reward",meaning:"The economic outcome of investing includes trading costs and applicable taxes. Contract notes and account statements document transactions and charges. Tax treatment depends on instrument, activity, holding period, taxpayer circumstances and the law applicable to the transaction. A teaching calculator using one percentage cost is a simplification, not a tax return calculation.",why:"Small per-trade friction compounds with turnover. Mixing gross results with after-cost results distorts comparison. Rules, rates and reporting forms change, so verify the current official guidance and record the date rather than relying on a remembered rate. This release intentionally does not hardcode tax rates or offer tax filing advice.",analogy:"A delivery business’s sales are not the owner’s take-home amount after fuel, wages and obligations. Investment gains also have a bridge from gross to net.",example:"Synthetic: a ₹1,000 gross gain less ₹120 transaction friction leaves ₹880 before any tax. The paper lab uses user-selected costs and slippage, not official broker charges.",formula:"Net before-tax P/L = gross P/L − transaction costs; after-tax outcome additionally depends on applicable tax treatment",exercise:"Add cost per trade in the expectancy lab. Keep a list of records needed to reconcile realised outcomes. Consult the official tax portal for current-year rules.",mistake:"Applying one remembered tax rate to every product or treating simulated costs as the actual broker charge schedule.",research:"Date-sensitive verification boundary: 10 October 2026. Use official Income Tax Department and exchange/broker documentation for actual obligations.",question:"The paper lab’s flat cost assumption represents:",options:["A simplified simulation input","An official tax calculation","All possible broker charges"],explanation:"It models friction for learning. Actual costs and taxes require current, transaction-specific information.",reference:"https://www.incometax.gov.in/iec/foportal/"},{level:11,title:"Investor protection and spotting scams",tool:"checklist",meaning:"Investment fraud often uses urgency, guaranteed returns, impersonation or requests to move money outside recognised channels. Verify intermediaries and complaints procedures through official sources. Registration is relevant to legitimacy but does not promise investment performance. Protect account credentials and verify the identity and authority of anyone asking for access.",why:"A polished app, social-media following or familiar logo can be forged. Price manipulation may use group tips and selected screenshots to create excitement. Do not reveal OTPs, passwords or trading PINs. Use official investor guidance and grievance channels, and preserve records if a suspicious transaction occurs.",analogy:"A convincing uniform does not establish someone’s identity. Checking the badge through an independent official directory is stronger evidence.",example:"Synthetic warning scenario: a message promises 3% every week and asks for a personal transfer to unlock withdrawals. Guaranteed claims and escalating payment requests require scrutiny, not a larger deposit.",formula:"Verification sequence: independent official source → identity and registration → authorised channel → documented terms",exercise:"Write three warning signs and independently locate SEBI’s investor-protection guidance. Do not follow a stranger’s supplied login link.",mistake:"Confusing a fake trading screen with proof of assets or believing a small initial withdrawal validates the whole scheme.",research:"Check primary filings and ownership of sources. The platform requests only an optional read-only Analytics Token entered in your own browser.",question:"Which claim is a warning sign?",options:["Guaranteed high returns with urgent payment demands","A clear statement of uncertainty","A link you independently opened on an official domain"],explanation:"Guaranteed-return marketing and pressure to transfer money are reasons to verify independently.",reference:l},{level:12,title:"Build an evidence-based company thesis",tool:"checklist",meaning:"A research thesis explains how a business works, why its economics may improve, what price assumptions are reasonable and what could go wrong. It should connect evidence to a falsifiable conclusion. Separate observed facts, derived calculations and estimates. A Watch, Buy, Hold or Sell thesis is your research stance, not an instruction generated by this platform.",why:"An explicit argument exposes missing information and makes later review meaningful. A strong company can still be overpriced; an inexpensive company can still deteriorate. Examine customers, competition, capital allocation, governance and financial resilience together. Listing attractive ratios is not the same as explaining their sustainability.",analogy:"A court argument needs evidence, a chain of reasoning and consideration of opposing facts. A slogan or a numerical score cannot replace that chain.",example:"Synthetic DemoCo thesis: recurring customers support revenue, but rising receivables could weaken cash conversion. The thesis is conditional on collections normalising and valuation allowing for slower growth.",formula:"Thesis structure = business case + financial evidence + valuation assumptions + risks + invalidation conditions",exercise:"Complete the research checklist, open Decision Lab and write one positive fact, one negative fact and one unanswered question. Save the note.",mistake:"Writing only a target price or treating missing data as positive evidence.",research:"Every important assertion should carry a dated source. Imported data provenance does not independently verify accuracy.",question:"What makes a thesis reviewable?",options:["Dated evidence and conditions that would invalidate it","A guaranteed price target","A single opaque score"],explanation:"Review needs observable conditions and an audit trail of what was believed when."},{level:12,title:"Peer comparisons and data quality",tool:"comparison",meaning:"Peer comparison evaluates businesses with similar economic drivers using consistent definitions, periods and units. It can reveal useful questions but cannot eliminate differences in quality, risk or growth. Data quality includes completeness, freshness, adjustment status and reporting scope. Missing values must remain unavailable rather than silently becoming zeros.",why:"A bank and an engineering company have different balance-sheet economics. Even direct peers can differ in customer concentration, geography and acquisition history. Reporting annual profit in crores alongside price in rupees without conversion creates huge calculation errors. Compare only metrics supported by compatible inputs.",analogy:"Comparing fuel efficiency is meaningful for similar vehicles under similar test conditions. Comparing one in miles and another in kilometres without conversion is misleading.",example:"Synthetic: ₹2 crore profit equals ₹2,00,00,000. Dividing it by 10 lakh shares gives EPS ₹20. Treating “2” as rupees instead produces a meaningless tiny EPS.",formula:"Compatible metric = same economic definition + same period basis + same currency/units + clear scope",exercise:"Import two synthetic financial templates with distinct company identifiers. Compare margins and debt ratios, and explicitly flag any sector or period mismatch.",mistake:"Inventing missing figures, mixing standalone and consolidated results, or using widget visuals as a private data API.",research:"Use the comparison view’s source labels and units. A dataset’s last record does not prove it is the latest published information.",question:"What should an absent financial metric become?",options:["Unavailable with an explanation","Zero","The peer average without a label"],explanation:"Missingness is itself information. Filling it silently fabricates evidence."},{level:12,title:"Bull, base, bear and an independent review",tool:"checklist",meaning:"Scenario analysis develops distinct plausible outcomes rather than disguising uncertainty in a single number. A bull case has favourable assumptions; a bear case examines adverse economics; a base case is your central working hypothesis. These are not probabilities unless you explicitly assign and justify probabilities. Independent review looks for weak links in the reasoning.",why:"Large outcome ranges reveal sensitivity to growth, margin, financing and valuation. Contradictory evidence deserves attention rather than being averaged into a reassuring score. A stale dataset may leave important recent events unknown. A research decision can legitimately be “insufficient evidence” or “watch” while questions remain unresolved.",analogy:"Planning a journey includes good weather, expected traffic and a road closure. Writing the detour before departure helps when the adverse case arrives.",example:"Synthetic: base growth 8%, bull 15% and bear −5% need distinct explanations about demand and cost. A research note records the sources, observation date and conditions that would change each assumption.",formula:"Review loop: observe → calculate → challenge → record → monitor → revise",exercise:"Write three scenarios in Decision Lab. Export the journal and ask whether a reader could reproduce each key calculation from the stated source.",mistake:"Calling the optimistic case expected return or assigning precise probabilities without evidence.",research:"Use saved notes to track changes in evidence. The platform supports your independent conclusions and does not issue personalised recommendations.",question:"What is a reasonable response to major missing evidence?",options:["Record the gap and withhold a firm conclusion","Assume the best case","Hide the missing fields"],explanation:"Honest uncertainty is preferable to a strong conclusion built on absent inputs."});const c=r.map((e,t)=>{const i=t%e.options.length,n=e.options.slice(i).concat(e.options.slice(0,i));return{...e,options:n,id:e.title.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g,"-").replace(/-$/,""),minutes:9,answer:((e.answer??0)-i+n.length)%n.length,reference:e.reference??l}}),u=e=>c.find(t=>t.id===e);export{d as a,u as g,c as l};
